  "final_text": "Over the last 3 weeks (26 Feb to 18 Mar, 21 nights) you averaged about **7.0 hours a night**. The quality scores in this data look unreliable, though.\n\n**Duration**\n- Your best nights were 8.1 h (3 Mar) and 8.0 h (10 Mar).\n- Your shortest were 5.7 h (17 Mar) and 5.9 h (7 Mar). Those are the only two nights under 6 h.\n- Last night (18 Mar) was 6.25 h. That's the third short night in a row after 6.2 h and 5.7 h on 16 and 17 Mar, so you're carrying some sleep debt right now.\n- From 26 Feb to 15 Mar, nights mostly ranged from 6.4 to 8 h. Shorter nights tended to come every 3 to 4 days.\n\n**Stages**\n- Deep sleep was about 17% (roughly 1 to 1.3 h).\n- REM sleep was about 20%.\n- Light sleep was about 63%.\n- Those percentages are typical, and they barely changed across the period.\n\n**Last night**\n- The sleep score was 74, the highest of the period.\n- Sleep stress was 14 and awake time was about 26 minutes.\n- Overnight HRV was 58.\n\n**Data quality**\n- For the 20 nights before last night, several values are identical every night: sleep score 63 (\"Fair\"), HRV 61, sleep stress 19, SpO₂ 94% (lowest 87%) and awake time 13 minutes. Real data doesn't behave like that, so this looks like a sync or placeholder problem.\n- I'd trust the sleep durations and the 18 Mar numbers. I wouldn't read anything into the \"flat Fair score\" or the HRV and stress values for the earlier nights.\n\nIf you have a hard session planned today, I can check your readiness and HRV trend, but keep in mind the HRV history may not be reliable.",
